{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '245,308p;390,465p;558,664p'\"","aggregated_output":"   245\t### 4.1 The format record\n   246\t\n   247\tA format record is a plain map held in linker-local state. B6 fixed three\n   248\tfunction slots; this revision replaces one, renames one, and adds three:\n   249\t\n   250\t```clojure\n   251\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code      ; the dispatch key\n   252\t :contract            \"b1\"                    ; contract revision name\n   253\t :identity-fn         (fn [value] identity)   ; mint: publish! only\n   254\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [identity value] boolean) ; step 3\n   255\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 2, per part\n   256\t :validate-fn         (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 4, whole\n   257\t :obligations-fn      (fn [value] [sym ...])        ; step 5a: free names\n   258\t :definitions-fn      (fn [value] #{sym ...})       ; step 5a: own defs\n   259\t :parts-fn            (fn [value] [address ...])}   ; step 2 worklist\n   260\t```\n   261\t\n   262\t- `:identity-fn` mints an identity from a payload. It is used by\n   263\t  `publish!` and never by verification.\n   264\t- `:identity-matches-fn` is the step-3 check, directed by the identity.\n   265\t  For H and R it is `(= identity (image-hash value))`, a contract-pinned\n   266\t  hash with one algorithm. For the storage-derived formats it is\n   267\t  `(jing/segment-matches? identity value)`, so a value minted under\n   268\t  `:sha256` while the default is `:blake3` verifies under the algorithm\n   269\t  the identity names and never fails spuriously.\n   270\t- `:row-defect-fn` validates one fetched part on its own, before any\n   271\t  address it references is enqueued: tag, arity, slot kinds, saturation.\n   272\t  For single-payload formats it is the whole validator, run early.\n   273\t- `:parts-fn` returns the storage addresses a fetched payload references\n   274\t  and without which it is incomplete. For the three single-payload\n   275\t  formats it returns nothing. For `:yin.ast/code` it returns the child\n   276\t  row ids of a row body.\n   277\t- `:obligations-fn` and `:definitions-fn` are **position-bearing\n   278\t  scanners** with one shared result shape:\n   279\t\n   280\t  ```clojure\n   281\t  ;; :obligations-fn  value -> [{:name sym :at position :in-body? b}]\n   282\t  ;; :definitions-fn  value -> [{:name sym :at position\n   283\t  ;;                             :conditional? b}]\n   284\t  ;; position: a tree occurrence path [root-address path] for\n   285\t  ;;           :yin.ast/code; a pc for the vector formats\n   286\t  ```\n   287\t\n   288\t  `:conditional?` is true when any `:if` branch, jump target range, or\n   289\t  lambda body encloses the definition; `:in-body?` is true when a\n   290\t  lambda body encloses the occurrence. Dominance between two positions\n   291\t  is decided by the linker from these fields and the format's order\n   292\t  (step 5a). For the AST record both scanners are Datalog over the\n   293\t  rows: `yin.vm/free-names` today returns a symbol set, and is\n   294\t  extended (or wrapped by an occurrence-scoped query over the same\n   295\t  rules) to return each occurrence's path; the definitions scanner is\n   296\t  the store-key query behind `yin.vm/ast-requirements` with the\n   297\t  binding row's path and its enclosing tags. For the vector formats\n   298\t  both are operand scans by pc (`:var` against `:store-put`,\n   299\t  `:load-free` against the `yin/def` call sites) with conditional\n   300\t  target ranges taken from the `:jump-if` and body operands. A format\n   301\t  whose scanner returns positions as `nil` is admitted and falls under\n   302\t  the conservative degradation of step 5a: every occurrence is\n   303\t  retained as an obligation.\n   304\t\n   305\t`fetch` and `step` contain no branch on `:format`. Adding a fifth backend\n   306\tis one more record.\n   307\t\n   308\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   390\t   validator runs over the complete value: for a tree, over the\n   391\t   assembled row set (`:id-resolves`, `:acyclic`, `:root-reachable` are\n   392\t   whole-tree rules that step 2's row-local check cannot run); for a\n   393\t   vector, over the vector; for a register image, including the\n   394\t   ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A validator that throws is\n   395\t   `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:validator-refused`.\n   396\t5. **Verify dependency closure.** Two halves.\n   397\t   - *5a, at the linker: obligations.* Two scans run over the verified\n   398\t     value. `:obligations-fn` yields the free-name occurrences, each\n   399\t     with its position: for a tree, the occurrence path and whether it\n   400\t     lies inside a `:lambda` body; for a vector, the pc and whether it\n   401\t     lies in a body rather than the main sequence. `:definitions-fn`\n   402\t     yields the **definition set** with positions: every constant store\n   403\t     key the image binds at module level (the section 7.7 store-key\n   404\t     query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`: `:vm/store-put` rows,\n   405\t     `:store-put` operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key),\n   406\t     each with the position of the binding in the main sequence.\n   407\t     A definition discharges an occurrence by **defined-before-use\n   408\t     under control-flow dominance**, not by subtraction of names and\n   409\t     not by source order alone. Source order says a definition is\n   410\t     written earlier; dominance says it has executed by the time the\n   411\t     occurrence runs. Only the second discharges, and where dominance\n   412\t     cannot be established the obligation is **retained**, never\n   413\t     dropped:\n   414\t     - A definition is *unconditional* when it sits in the main\n   415\t       sequence at top level or inside a `:do` (for a tree) or at a pc\n   416\t       in the main sequence outside every conditional target range\n   417\t       (for a vector); it is *conditional* when any `:if` branch, any\n   418\t       jump target, or any lambda body encloses it. A conditional\n   419\t       definition discharges nothing statically: the linker cannot know\n   420\t       the branch was taken, so an occurrence that depends on it keeps\n   421\t       its obligation and is checked at the receiver under 5b exactly\n   422\t       as an undefined name would be, and the manifest must declare it\n   423\t       or the link is `:undeclared-free`. This is conservative by\n   424\t       design: a module that defines a name only in one branch and\n   425\t       reads it afterwards must declare what the name means when the\n   426\t       branch is not taken.\n   427\t     - An occurrence in the main sequence (outside every lambda body)\n   428\t       is discharged only by an unconditional definition of that name\n   429\t       that dominates it: for a tree, an earlier sibling in the same\n   430\t       or an enclosing top-level `:do`; for a vector, an earlier pc in\n   431\t       the main sequence with no conditional target range between the\n   432\t       two. A main-sequence occurrence whose only definitions are all\n   433\t       later or all conditional and later is `:use-before-definition`:\n   434\t       in the isolated store it would read nothing, and no manifest\n   435\t       declaration may stand in for a name the module itself defines\n   436\t       unconditionally later. An occurrence whose only definitions are\n   437\t       conditional and earlier retains its obligation, per the rule\n   438\t       above.\n   439\t     - An occurrence inside a lambda body is discharged by an\n   440\t       unconditional definition of that name anywhere in the main\n   441\t       sequence. The body runs only when the closure is applied; an\n   442\t       application in the main sequence is itself a main-sequence\n   443\t       occurrence the rules above order, and an application after\n   444\t       export happens after halt, when every unconditional definition\n   445\t       has run. A body applied in the main sequence before a name it\n   446\t       reads is defined is not caught here; it fails in the child at\n   447\t       run time as an ordinary error, and section 7.3's `refused` phase\n   448\t       reports it.\n   449\t     Where a scanner cannot compute positions at all, the rule\n   450\t     degrades to the conservative side: every occurrence keeps its\n   451\t     obligation and no definition discharges anything. Under that\n   452\t     degradation a self-defining module still links only if its\n   453\t     manifest declares every name it reads, which is more obligation\n   454\t     than necessary and never less.\n   455\t     Each undischarged occurrence is joined with the manifest that\n   456\t     delivered the image (section 8.1): a name under\n   457\t     `:yin.module/primitives` becomes\n   458\t     `{:name n :kind :primitive :profile addr}`; a name exported by a\n   459\t     module under `:yin.module/requires` becomes\n   460\t     `{:name n :kind :module :module m :manifest addr}`. A free name\n   461\t     the manifest declares nowhere is `:undeclared-free`; the publisher\n   462\t     did not say what the name means, so no receiver can bind it\n   463\t     identically. The definition set is **advisory for exports**: an\n   464\t     export under `:yin.module/exports` that the static scan finds is\n   465\t     known to be bound if the body halts, and one it does not find\n   558\t## 5. Format records for the four backends\n   559\t\n   560\tThree records bind existing code; the AST record's free-name scanner is\n   561\tthe section 4.5 query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, implemented as\n   562\t`yin.vm/free-names`.\n   563\t\n   564\t### 5.1 `:yin.ast/code` (the AST walker)\n   565\t\n   566\t```clojure\n   567\t{:format              :yin.ast/code\n   568\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   569\t :identity-fn         (fn [body] (jing/segment-key body))\n   570\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [root-id body] (jing/segment-matches? root-id\n   571\t                                                                  body))\n   572\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [body] (row-local-defect body))\n   573\t :validate-fn         (fn [tree] (yin.vm/validate-rows tree))\n   574\t :obligations-fn      (fn [tree] (yin.vm/free-names (relation tree)\n   575\t                                                    (occurrences tree)\n   576\t                                                    (:root tree)))\n   577\t :definitions-fn      (fn [tree] (store-put-keys (relation tree)))\n   578\t :parts-fn            (fn [body] (body-child-ids body))}\n   579\t```\n   580\t\n   581\tPayload: one row body `[tag & slots]` per address, exactly as\n   582\t`yin.vm.content/materialize-tree!` stores it (D3: individual rows, no pack\n   583\tformat; the body is what is hashed, and the id is derived from it). The\n   584\tidentity is the root row's id. Steps 2 and 3 therefore hash the fetched\n   585\tbody as received; the address is prepended only when the linker assembles\n   586\t`{id [id tag & slots]}` for step 4, the shape `validate-rows` takes.\n   587\t`row-local-defect` runs the `:tag`, `:arity`, `:slot-kind`, and\n   588\t`:saturation` rules of section 7.4 over one body, so a malformed row\n   589\tnever has its slots followed. Step 2's worklist follows `node` and\n   590\t`nodes` slots by the grammar's positions, the walk\n   591\t`yin.vm.content/load-rows` performs today; that function's throwing\n   592\tsurface is retired in favor of the linker's refusals (section 9).\n   593\tFree names are the conservative, occurrence-scoped set. Loading is\n   594\t`yin.vm.ast-walker/vm-load-rows`.\n   595\t\n   596\t### 5.2 `:yin.semantic/code` (the semantic VM)\n   597\t\n   598\t```clojure\n   599\t{:format              :yin.semantic/code\n   600\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   601\t :identity-fn         jing/segment-key\n   602\t :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?\n   603\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   604\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   605\t :obligations-fn      semantic-free-names\n   606\t :definitions-fn      semantic-definitions\n   607\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   608\t```\n   609\t\n   610\tPayload: the canonical positional instruction tuple vector (UCF section\n   611\t7.3.2), stored as the exact vector at its own `segment-key`; nothing\n   612\telse may hash there (UCF section 7.3.4). The identity is that address.\n   613\t`well-formed-vector?` is the section 7.5 grammar. `semantic-free-names`\n   614\tscans `:var` operands not bound by an enclosing closure's params, the\n   615\t`$code` query of section 7.7; the linker module defines it, as B6\n   616\tdefined the two de Bruijn scanners. The contract stamp is UCF's\n   617\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v2\"}`; a link request for this format carries the\n   618\tstamp, and the receiver's index is keyed per stamp (UCF section 7.3.3).\n   619\tLoading is `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`, which writes the address to the\n   620\timage and the alias column.\n   621\t\n   622\t### 5.3 `:yin.debruijn.code` (the stack VM)\n   623\t\n   624\t```clojure\n   625\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code\n   626\t :contract            \"b1\"\n   627\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   628\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [H v] (= H (yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash v)))\n   629\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   630\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   631\t :obligations-fn      stack-free-names\n   632\t :definitions-fn      stack-definitions\n   633\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   634\t```\n   635\t\n   636\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.1 in substance. The identity H is\n   637\t`image-hash` over the descriptor hash (which embeds the lowering contract\n   638\tversion) and the canonical vector; the `=` here compares two values of\n   639\tone contract-pinned hash and is not a Class 3 site. Free names are\n   640\t`:load-free` operands. Loading is `yin.vm.debruijn.stack/load-image`;\n   641\t`yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` shows the relocation a second image\n   642\tneeds beside a held one.\n   643\t\n   644\t### 5.4 `:yin.debruijn.register` (the register VM)\n   645\t\n   646\t```clojure\n   647\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.register\n   648\t :contract            \"r1\"\n   649\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   650\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [R v]\n   651\t                        (= R (yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   652\t                               v)))\n   653\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   654\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   655\t :obligations-fn      register-free-names\n   656\t :definitions-fn      register-definitions\n   657\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   658\t```\n   659\t\n   660\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.2 in substance. Validation includes the\n   661\tlive-set rules; free names are `[:load-free rd name]` operands across all\n   662\tbodies. Loading is `yin.vm.debruijn.register/load-image`.\n   663\t\n   664\t### 5.5 Same-root pairing becomes derivation verification\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
