{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1040,1208p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1040\t  coordinates are meaningless in the parent, so bindings cross the\n  1041\t  child-to-parent boundary the way a continuation crosses any boundary:\n  1042\t  by lift and lower. The `linked` transition is four acts in order.\n  1043\t  1. *The child's export slice is lifted* to UCF's portable encoding\n  1044\t     (section 7.5.1, amended below): each closure becomes a\n  1045\t     `:yin.k/closure` marker naming its **origin image** and its entry\n  1046\t     relative to that image, with its captured values encoded\n  1047\t     recursively; every other value encodes by the same grammar. The\n  1048\t     origin is not assumed to be the module's own image: a closure the\n  1049\t     child obtained from a dependency and re-exported originates in\n  1050\t     that dependency's image, and the lift records whichever image the\n  1051\t     closure's coordinates fall in. The lift is refused as\n  1052\t     `:yin.k/non-portable` for the same leaves UCF refuses (a host\n  1053\t     object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift refuses the\n  1054\t     install: an export that cannot cross a boundary is not an export.\n  1055\t  2. *The origin set is checked.* The lifted slice's\n  1056\t     `:yin.k/requires :yin.k/segments` enumerates every origin image it\n  1057\t     names, transitively through captured values. Each must be an image\n  1058\t     this scheduler verified: the module's own, or one delivered for an\n  1059\t     install in this scheduler's history (held under `:installs` or the\n  1060\t     registry by identity). An origin identity the scheduler never\n  1061\t     verified is `:foreign-image`, and the install is refused; a\n  1062\t     verified image is never re-fetched for this step.\n  1063\t  3. *The parent attaches every origin image it does not yet hold*,\n  1064\t     non-destructively. The ordinary loaders (`vm-load-rows`,\n  1065\t     `load-vector`, `load-image`) are entry points for a fresh run:\n  1066\t     they set the program counter, control, or root, and reset the\n  1067\t     operand stack and frames. The parent at `linked` is a parked task\n  1068\t     whose registers must survive, so each kernel exposes a second\n  1069\t     operation, **`attach-image`**, that extends the kernel's code\n  1070\t     space and touches nothing else. Its contract, per kernel:\n  1071\t     - *semantic*: the verified vector is added under a fresh local id\n  1072\t       and the address is written to the alias column; `:control`,\n  1073\t       `:env`, `:stack`, `:k`, every `:return` frame's segment and pc,\n  1074\t       and the store are unchanged, because existing local ids are\n  1075\t       never renumbered (UCF section 7.3.4, \"local-id immutability\n  1076\t       stands\");\n  1077\t     - *stack* and *register*: the image is relocated by the current\n  1078\t       held length and appended to `:segment` (and, for the register\n  1079\t       VM, `:bodies` shifted likewise), and one `[identity offset\n  1080\t       length]` row is appended to the **offset table**, a kernel-state\n  1081\t       vector the lift of act 1 also reads; `:pc`, `:stack`, `:frames`,\n  1082\t       `:continuation` (every `:return-pc` and `:frames` in it), and\n  1083\t       `:registers` are unchanged, because appending never moves an\n  1084\t       instruction already held; `:hash` becomes the identity of the\n  1085\t       new concatenation, as `yin.repl`'s `append-*-image` computes it;\n  1086\t     - *walker*: the verified tree's rows are added to the kernel's\n  1087\t       row set (`:code-segment` expansion) keyed by their ids; the\n  1088\t       current node, environment, and continuation are unchanged.\n  1089\t     `yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` and `append-register-image`\n  1090\t     already do the relocation and append but then set `:pc` to the\n  1091\t     new offset to start the next input; `attach-image` is that\n  1092\t     function without the final `:pc` assignment, and `yin.repl` is\n  1093\t     rewritten to call `attach-image` and then set `:pc` itself. A\n  1094\t     parked task attached to in this way resumes at the same pc, with\n  1095\t     the same stack and frames, and every restore identity it holds\n  1096\t     (`:return-pc`, `:stack-base`, semantic `:segment` ids, cursor\n  1097\t     cells) still names what it named. An image already present is not\n  1098\t     attached twice; the alias column or the offset table says so.\n  1099\t  4. *The parent lowers the encoding* against its own coordinate space\n  1100\t     by the mapping below. The lowered slice is what the registry value\n  1101\t     gains as `:bindings`.\n  1102\t  The registry value then gains `{'foo {:manifest m :address a\n  1103\t  :derivation d :bindings lowered}}`, `resolve-var`'s module step reads\n  1104\t  `:bindings`, and every waiter is restored with `'foo` as its value.\n  1105\t\n  1106\t  **The closure marker, and its lift and lower per binding discipline.**\n  1107\t  UCF section 7.5.1 defines the closure marker for the named semantic\n  1108\t  VM. The de Bruijn kernels are positional and nameless, so the marker\n  1109\t  gains a `:yin.k/binding` key with two variants, which is an amendment\n  1110\t  to UCF listed in section 11, item 12:\n  1111\t\n  1112\t  ```clojure\n  1113\t  ;; named (:yin.ast/code, :yin.semantic/code)\n  1114\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :named\n  1115\t   :yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc\n  1116\t   :yin.k/params [sym ...] :yin.k/env {sym encoded}}\n  1117\t\n  1118\t  ;; positional (:yin.debruijn.code, :yin.debruijn.register)\n  1119\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :positional\n  1120\t   :yin.k/segment identity :yin.k/entry pc\n  1121\t   :yin.k/arity n :yin.k/frames [[encoded ...] ...]}\n  1122\t  ```\n  1123\t\n  1124\t  | Kernel closure                       | Lift                          |\n  1125\t  |--------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n  1126\t  | semantic `{:type :closure :params p  | `addr` = alias column inverse |\n  1127\t  | :entry e :segment local :env E}`     | of `local`; `pc` = `e`;       |\n  1128\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1129\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1130\t  | walker `{:type :closure :params p    | `addr` = the row id of the    |\n  1131\t  | :body node :env E}` (the body is an  | `:lambda` row whose body slot |\n  1132\t  | AST node, not a code coordinate)     | is `node`, found by the walk  |\n  1133\t  |                                      | below; `pc` = `nil`;          |\n  1134\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1135\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1136\t  | stack / register `{:type :closure    | offset-table entry with       |\n  1137\t  | :arity n :body-pc p :frames F}`      | `off <= p < off + len` gives  |\n  1138\t  |                                      | `identity` and `pc = p - off`;|\n  1139\t  |                                      | `arity` = `n`; `frames` =     |\n  1140\t  |                                      | `mapv (mapv encode) F`,       |\n  1141\t  |                                      | outermost frame first         |\n  1142\t\n  1143\t  | Marker variant | Lower into the parent                              |\n  1144\t  |----------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n  1145\t  | `:named`,      | `local'` = parent alias column at `addr`; `:entry` |\n  1146\t  | semantic       | unchanged; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =           |\n  1147\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1148\t  | `:named`,      | `:body` = the body slot of the parent's row at     |\n  1149\t  | walker         | `addr`, reconstructed as a node by the walker's    |\n  1150\t  |                | row decoder; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =         |\n  1151\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1152\t  | `:positional`  | `off'` = parent offset-table entry for `identity`; |\n  1153\t  |                | `:body-pc` = `off' + pc`; `:arity` = `n`;          |\n  1154\t  |                | `:frames` = `mapv (mapv decode) frames`            |\n  1155\t\n  1156\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\n  1157\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\n  1158\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of exactly one\n  1159\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\n  1160\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The lift\n  1161\t  finds it by the walker's own row index (`node -> row id`, which\n  1162\t  `vm-load-rows` builds when it decodes rows into nodes and which\n  1163\t  `attach-image` extends), and refuses the lift as\n  1164\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :unrooted-body` when the node\n  1165\t  is in no held row, which can happen only for a closure a host\n  1166\t  function fabricated. The origin image of a walker closure is the\n  1167\t  tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda` row id is\n  1168\t  content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two trees is one id\n  1169\t  and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads the parent's row at\n  1170\t  that id (present after act 3) and decodes its body slot into a node\n  1171\t  through the same decoder the loader uses, so the closure's `:body`\n  1172\t  in the parent is structurally equal to the child's and its `:params`\n  1173\t  and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\n  1174\t\n  1175\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\n  1176\t  marker only into a positional kernel of the same `:format`; the\n  1177\t  reverse is `:binding-mismatch`. This never arises within one install,\n  1178\t  because the child is a VM of the same backend as the parent (section\n  1179\t  7.3, first rule); it is stated so that a lifted slice carried\n  1180\t  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\n  1181\t  closure between disciplines is a re-lowering of source under a\n  1182\t  derivation record (section 8.1), never a relocation. `decode` and\n  1183\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\n  1184\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\n  1185\t  origin set of act 2.\n  1186\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1187\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1188\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1189\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1190\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1191\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\n  1192\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1193\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1194\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1195\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1196\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1197\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1198\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n  1199\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n  1200\t  again.\n  1201\t\n  1202\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n  1203\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n  1204\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n  1205\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n  1206\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n  1207\tgrain.\n  1208\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
