{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1135,1207p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1135\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1136\t  | stack / register `{:type :closure    | offset-table entry with       |\n  1137\t  | :arity n :body-pc p :frames F}`      | `off <= p < off + len` gives  |\n  1138\t  |                                      | `identity` and `pc = p - off`;|\n  1139\t  |                                      | `arity` = `n`; `frames` =     |\n  1140\t  |                                      | `mapv (mapv encode) F`,       |\n  1141\t  |                                      | outermost frame first         |\n  1142\t\n  1143\t  | Marker variant | Lower into the parent                              |\n  1144\t  |----------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n  1145\t  | `:named`,      | `local'` = parent alias column at `addr`; `:entry` |\n  1146\t  | semantic       | unchanged; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =           |\n  1147\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1148\t  | `:named`,      | `:body` = the body slot of the parent's row at     |\n  1149\t  | walker         | `addr`, reconstructed as a node by the walker's    |\n  1150\t  |                | row decoder; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =         |\n  1151\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1152\t  | `:positional`  | `off'` = parent offset-table entry for `identity`; |\n  1153\t  |                | `:body-pc` = `off' + pc`; `:arity` = `n`;          |\n  1154\t  |                | `:frames` = `mapv (mapv decode) frames`            |\n  1155\t\n  1156\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\n  1157\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\n  1158\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of exactly one\n  1159\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\n  1160\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The lift\n  1161\t  finds it by the walker's own row index (`node -> row id`, which\n  1162\t  `vm-load-rows` builds when it decodes rows into nodes and which\n  1163\t  `attach-image` extends), and refuses the lift as\n  1164\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :unrooted-body` when the node\n  1165\t  is in no held row, which can happen only for a closure a host\n  1166\t  function fabricated. The origin image of a walker closure is the\n  1167\t  tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda` row id is\n  1168\t  content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two trees is one id\n  1169\t  and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads the parent's row at\n  1170\t  that id (present after act 3) and decodes its body slot into a node\n  1171\t  through the same decoder the loader uses, so the closure's `:body`\n  1172\t  in the parent is structurally equal to the child's and its `:params`\n  1173\t  and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\n  1174\t\n  1175\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\n  1176\t  marker only into a positional kernel of the same `:format`; the\n  1177\t  reverse is `:binding-mismatch`. This never arises within one install,\n  1178\t  because the child is a VM of the same backend as the parent (section\n  1179\t  7.3, first rule); it is stated so that a lifted slice carried\n  1180\t  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\n  1181\t  closure between disciplines is a re-lowering of source under a\n  1182\t  derivation record (section 8.1), never a relocation. `decode` and\n  1183\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\n  1184\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\n  1185\t  origin set of act 2.\n  1186\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1187\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1188\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1189\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1190\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1191\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\n  1192\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1193\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1194\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1195\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1196\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1197\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1198\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n  1199\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n  1200\t  again.\n  1201\t\n  1202\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n  1203\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n  1204\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n  1205\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n  1206\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n  1207\tgrain.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
